  "summary": "Recent preprints link tissue mechanics and centrosome-generated forces to morphogenesis, division timing, and axial organ geometry.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is that physical forces actively organize developmental outcomes. In Drosophila wing disc eversion, a multiscale model paired with imaging places extracellular matrix detachment, boundary forces, and actomyosin contraction in a defined sequence. In Drosophila neural stem cells, centrosome identity is reported to create asymmetric microtubule-dependent pushing forces that position the basal spindle and set daughter cell size. A separate Drosophila gastrulation preprint suggests that mesoderm invagination and embryo-wide force transmission influence the timing of mitotic entry. The mouse notochord study extends this logic to vertebrate axis elongation, where imaging and a biophysical model indicate that a proliferation gradient maintains constant cross-section. The emphasis across these items is on linking force transmission to cell cycle timing and tissue geometry, though the items differ in organism and scale.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
